# Deep beginner lessons, part 2: Q16-Q29.
$LEARN[16] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Trunk-based development means one shared <b>main</b> branch plus short-lived feature branches; main moves only through pull requests. Two settings implement that. The <b>branch protection rule</b> on main set to Enabled (require PR before merging, require approvals) makes direct pushes impossible: every change, human or agent, arrives as a PR. The workflow <b>trigger on: pull_request</b> makes validation automatic: each opened or updated PR runs tests and lint, and merging stays blocked until green. The agent keeps full autonomy to create branches and open PRs: protection constrains main, never the agent workspace. <b>Example:</b> the agent pushes three commits to copilot/fix-login. Each push triggers validation; reviewers see green checks plus one required approval; only then does Squash and merge unlock. on: push would run on direct pushes (which protection forbids anyway), schedule/dispatch would validate at random times instead of gating each PR.</div>"
$LEARN[17] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Copilot usage is metered in <b>tokens</b> (roughly word pieces the model reads and writes), and premium models and long agentic sessions consume budget fast. When local behavior must match the cloud agent run, you reproduce in the CLI and need to know where the budget stands. The CLI exposes one command per chore: <b>/usage</b> prints current token consumption, <b>/compact</b> shrinks context, <b>/diff</b> shows uncommitted changes, <b>/context</b> shows loaded context. <b>Example:</b> mid-reproduction you run /usage and see premium requests nearly exhausted: you switch the remaining exploration to a cheaper model instead of hitting a wall mid-task. Reaching for /compact would summarize the session (useful, different chore), /diff would show files (different question), /context would show scope (different question). Name the chore, pick the slash.</div>"
$LEARN[18] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> An MCP client configuration entry has two load-bearing fields. <b>type</b> names the transport: <b>http</b> for a hosted HTTPS endpoint (a URL you call), <b>stdio</b> for a local process the client spawns, <b>local</b> for on-machine servers. The GitHub MCP lives at a URL, so it is http. <b>Authorization</b> carries credentials, and GitHub personal access tokens use the <b>Bearer</b> scheme: the literal word Bearer, a space, then the token (here read from the GITHUB_PAT environment variable so the secret never lands in the file). <b>Example entry:</b> github server, type http, url the copilot MCP endpoint, headers Authorization Bearer plus the PAT variable. Swap in stdio and the client tries to spawn a local process that does not exist. Swap in Token or x-api-key and GitHub rejects the scheme with authentication errors. Transport must match where the server lives; scheme must match what the server speaks.</div>"
$LEARN[19] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The <b>copilot agent run</b> command launches headless agents with two opposite flags. <b>--allow-tool</b> names tools that run without approval prompts: velocity for safe operations. <b>--deny-tool</b> removes tools entirely: they cannot run even with approval. Tools you neither allow nor deny keep working but keep prompting: available yet supervised. The requirement has two halves pulling in opposite directions: read-only tasks must fly (no prompts on read/search), high-risk tools must stay usable but supervised (edit/execute available, approval-gated). <b>Example:</b> with --allow-tool 'read,search', a triage sweep across 200 files never pauses, while the first edit still stops and asks. Add --deny-tool 'edit,execute' and that first edit becomes impossible instead of supervised: the agent reports it cannot complete the task. --allow-all-tools removes every gate including the safety ones. Match each requirement half to the flag that serves exactly it.</div>"
$LEARN[20] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> This workflow standardizes agent PR quality gates: unit tests plus security scanning. The test job runs the repo suite with <b>npm test</b> because this is a Node repository (a Python repo would use its own runner; build would only compile). Security scanning uses the GitHub <b>CodeQL action</b>, which always runs as an ordered pair: <b>init</b> builds the code database (understanding the code), then <b>analyze</b> executes the security queries against it (judging the code). Reversing or skipping one breaks the pair. <b>upload-sarif</b> belongs to a different story: importing results from third-party scanners into GitHub code scanning. With the native CodeQL action there is no SARIF file to upload; init plus analyze already delivers findings to the security tab. Read each box as asking which phase of which tool, then place the phase name.</div>"
$LEARN[21] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> After the coding agent opens a PR, reviewers rarely accept the first draft: they request changes. Those follow-ups must reach the <b>same agent session</b> (which holds all context) rather than starting a fresh one. GitHub routes by mention and place: commenting <b>@copilot inside the PR comments</b> addresses the session that owns the PR. Two preconditions make it work. The commenter needs <b>write access</b>: without it GitHub ignores the instruction. And the mention must be <b>@copilot</b> (the agent), in the <b>PR</b> (its workplace): @github elsewhere addresses different machinery. <b>Example:</b> 'please also handle empty carts, @copilot' under the PR triggers a new commit on the same branch. Unassigning and reassigning the issue would spawn a blank session that redoes discovery; a fresh Chat task is disconnected from the PR entirely. Route, do not restart.</div>"
$LEARN[22] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> This scenario packs three independent mini-lessons. First, a planner profile with only <b>search/read/fetch</b> plus written orders never to write code is explicitly fenced: no edit tool exists and instructions forbid writing, so any code appearing in the same conversation is model non-compliance, not configuration intent. Second, web access via the allowed <b>fetch</b> tool is legitimate: firewall-blocked outbound shell commands are an environment problem, and deleting <b>search</b> only blinds codebase lookup without touching network behavior. Third, a <b>repo-level copilot-instructions.md</b> applies to every agent run in the repo, so a 'run tests and linters before pushing' rule fires on every PR and directly starves the failing-CI stall pattern. <b>Example fix chain:</b> keep the planner fenced, allowlist the firewall for the runner, add the instructions file: each fix targets exactly one statement, which is why the answers are Yes, No, Yes.</div>"
$LEARN[23] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI reads repository-scoped hooks from <b>.github/hooks/*.json</b>. Each hook type watches a different moment. <b>preToolUse</b> intercepts a tool or Bash command before it runs and can allow, prompt, or deny it via <b>permissionDecision</b>: the enforcement point. <b>userPromptSubmitted</b> sees your prompt text (good for logging, useless for gating commands, and a nonzero exit kills the whole session). <b>sessionStart</b> runs once at launch: banners, not enforcement. <b>Example policy:</b> a preToolUse hook receives every Bash command, auto-allows npm test and git status, and returns deny for anything matching sudo, rm -rf / or a curl pipe into bash. Low-risk work flows untouched, high-risk commands die before executing. Logging prompts or printing a banner never stops a command: only a pre-execution decision point can.</div>"
$LEARN[24] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> VS Code discovers custom agents from two places with a visibility rule each. <b>Workspace agents</b> come from <b>.github/agents/*.agent.md on the currently checked-out branch</b>: switch branches and the set changes, because different branches contain different files. The dropdown shows each under its file-derived display name. <b>Profile agents</b> come from your user profile across all repos, but only if <b>user-invokable</b> is not false: false hides the agent everywhere, so it can neither appear nor override a same-named workspace agent. <b>Example:</b> on main the picker lists release-notes (file present). Check out branch2 where the file was never committed: the entry vanishes, because discovery reads the branch, not your memory. Flip to profile1 whose copy says user-invokable: false: nothing appears and nothing overrides. Branch controls workspace visibility; the flag controls profile visibility.</div>"
$LEARN[25] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Rolling back an agent-profile change must be one revert plus one rerun, which demands the workflow deploy <b>exactly the ref you name</b> and read the profile <b>from the repo at that ref</b>. A <b>workflow_dispatch input</b> (here ref: commit SHA, tag or branch) carries your choice into the run as <b>inputs.ref</b>. Checkout with ref inputs.ref materializes that exact commit; validating the profile path and deploying from it keeps everything pinned to the same snapshot. Contrast <b>github.ref</b>: the ref that triggered the workflow run itself (usually main), which silently deploys the wrong commit. And workspace agent profiles conventionally live under <b>.github/agents/</b>, so that path prefix is load-bearing too. <b>Example:</b> dispatch with ref abc123, find a bad prompt, git revert the commit, rerun with the parent SHA: two clicks, fully deterministic, because no step ever resolved 'latest' on its own.</div>"
$LEARN[26] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> <b>Copilot Memory</b> stores two things: <b>repository-level facts</b> (conventions, build commands, architecture: shared with everyone having memory access in that repo) and <b>user-level preferences</b> (your personal style: only yours, across repos). Two documented deletion paths exist. <b>Manual deletion:</b> repo owners/admins review and delete repo facts under Settings, Copilot, Memory; users delete their own preferences in personal settings. <b>Automatic expiry:</b> any entry unused for 28 days is deleted (each successful use resets the clock). <b>Example:</b> a fact 'deploy via ./scripts/ship.sh' nobody triggers for a month quietly disappears; an obsolete fact you spot today you delete by hand in settings. Archiving a repo deletes nothing by itself (inactivity just lets the 28-day rule bite), deleting source code merely makes the fact ignored at validation time, and merging PRs never deletes: in fact closed-unmerged PRs can create memories.</div>"
$LEARN[27] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Copilot Chat answers from two context sources: <b>conversation history</b> (everything said so far) and <b>open files / working set</b> (what the model may treat as relevant code). Old troubleshooting turns pollute history: the model keeps borrowing assumptions from a dead problem. Deleting memories destroys durable repo knowledge to fix a transient context problem. Deleting one request is fiddly and never fully resets. Switching to agent mode changes behavior, not context. <b>Example:</b> after an hour debugging flaky timeouts, you pivot to writing docs. The model keeps suggesting retry logic because the history screams timeouts. Start a new chat: one click, clean history, memories intact. Then close the timeout files and open only the docs: every future answer now draws on the docs instead. Reset history plus curate the working set: the complete focus recipe.</div>"
$LEARN[28] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> MCP servers come in two flavors with opposite portability. <b>Local servers</b> (Docker containers, CLIs added via /mcp add) live on one laptop: configured fast, but gone the moment you open github.com on a tablet. <b>GitHub-hosted MCP servers</b> live in the cloud attached to your account: identical toolset in VS Code and on github.com with zero local setup. Orthogonal to hosting, the <b>organization Copilot policy</b> is the central switch enabling MCP servers across every Copilot environment at once. <b>Example:</b> a team puts its ticket-system MCP on a hosted server and flips the policy on. A developer starts triage in VS Code, continues from a phone browser at lunch with the same ticket tools, and IT never configured a laptop. Docker would strand the tools on one machine; /mcp add would repeat manual setup per device; changing Chat mode changes nothing about tool availability.</div>"
$LEARN[29] = "<div class=`"lesson`"><b>Learn it properly:</b> A silent agent is not necessarily a dead agent, so confirm before acting. <b>View session</b> (from the PR) streams the live session: running, waiting on approvals, or genuinely stuck. If it needs a push, comment <b>@copilot on the PR</b>: the same session continues with its completed steps, chosen approach and branch intact. The alternatives all destroy continuity: closing/reopening the issue, opening a fresh PR from Chat, or marking the draft ready all start over or misrepresent state; repo insights and local git log show code, never agent state. <b>Example:</b> status shows 'started work' then 55 minutes of silence. View session reveals it waiting on a workflow approval: click Approve and run, then comment '@copilot continue after CI' if needed. Observe first through the session, steer second through the PR: resume without redo.</div>"
